                "explanation": "<strong>A</strong> <p>The area of a rectangle\r                                 is calculated by multiplying the base, <em>x</em>, by the height,\r                                     <img src=\"images/2e44037e142d41d1a43ad304bbcb5eeb.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\">. This gives <img src=\"images/9ff47c485f2941d4a0c875ab238cfeb6.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\">, which is the formula you are trying to optimize. Start by\r                                 differentiating and identifying all critical values.</p> <p><img src=\"images/252f39a0881c420cabc043c5583ba370.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\"></p> <p>Having a negative\r                                     <em>x</em>-value is unreasonable for the context of the problem,\r                                 making <img src=\"images/5dae8a90ffe44f74bd63a450cda6e525.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\"> the only critical value. Using test intervals will show that\r                                     <em>A</em><em>′</em> transitions from positive to negative at <img src=\"images/530bf83656ff445a8257e36d3e6e368e.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\">, which means there is a maximum at that <em>x</em>-value.</p> <p>Thus, (A) is correct.\r                                 (B) is an <em>x</em>-intercept of the area function, but that value\r                                 would leave a height of 0 and an area of 0. (C) is the maximum area,\r                                 but this question is focused on the <em>x</em>-value.</p>"
