                "answer": "D",
                "explanation": "<strong>D</strong> <p>When trying to identify\r                                 local maximum or minimum values, apply the first derivative test.\r                                 For a local maximum to exist, the rate of change of the function,\r                                     <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>), must change from increasing to decreasing.\r                                 This means the derivative transitions from a positive value to a\r                                 negative value. This transition can take place only when the\r                                 derivative is equal to 0 or is undefined. The values where the\r                                 derivative is equal to 0 or undefined are called critical values. In\r                                 this problem, the derivative is provided. By setting it equal to 0\r                                 you get critical values of <em>x</em> = –2, <em>x</em> = 1, and <em>x</em>\r                                 = 5. There are no <em>x</em>-values that will make the derivative\r                                 undefined. To determine if any of these critical values are local\r                                 maximums, you must consider the surrounding intervals.</p>    Test\r                                             Interval Sign of\r                                                 <em>f′</em>(<em>x</em>)     (–∞,\r                                             –2) +   (–2,\r                                             1) +   (1, 5) –   (5, ∞) +    <p>The derivative changes\r                                 from positive to negative at <em>x</em> = 1, making it the only local\r                                 maximum on <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>).</p> <p>Therefore, <strong>(D)</strong> is\r                                 correct. There is no sign change at <em>x</em> = –2. The change at\r                                     <em>x</em> = 5 is from negative to positive, which means\r                                     <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) has a local minimum at this point. (A) lists\r                                 the critical values. (C) would be correct if the question was asking\r                                 for a local minimum on <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>).</p>"
